        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is structurally clean, additive, and consistent with the codebase's established patterns.\n\n- **Right unit.** `Owner.customerCode(...)` joins the existing family of pure static derivation methods on `Owner` (`householdId`, `identityKey`, `membershipNumber`, `membershipLevel`, `locality`, `regionForPostcode`). Previously the computation lived in the service only because it needed the repository for the per-city sequence; now that the sequence is gone, moving the pure derivation into the model is exactly the natural home, and `ClinicServiceImpl.generateCustomerCode` reduces to a thin delegating wrapper that keeps the `saveOwner` flow readable and symmetric with the other generators.\n\n- **Duplication removed, not added.** Extracting `sha256Hex` as a shared private primitive is a genuine improvement: `householdId` and the new `customerCode` now agree on algorithm/encoding instead of each open-coding the digest-to-hex loop. The Javadoc correctly frames it as the shared primitive.\n\n- **No dead code left behind.** `CITY_REGION`/`regionForCity` are retained because `isPostcodeValidForCity` still uses them, and the diff honestly repurposes their doc comments to reflect the narrowed role. `Objects` is still used in several other filters, so no stale import. `regionForPostcode` is now shared between validation and `customerCode`.\n\n- **Coherent ripple.** Everything built from the customer code moves with it without extra plumbing: `membershipNumber` already takes `customerCode` and just needed its example updated; the create audit record reads `owner.getCustomerCode()` and so picks up the new identity automatically; `locality` is re-expressed as a trivial parse of the code's region segment and the mapper/OpenAPI docs are updated in lockstep.\n\nThe one nit \u2014 the `\"UNKNOWN\"` literal now appears in `customerCode`, `regionForCity`, and `locality` \u2014 predates this change and is too minor to warrant blocking."
      }
